{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And of Levi he said</b> — \"he said concerning Levi\"; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:8), and so the opinion of the commentators. It is like the language of אִמְרִי לִי אָחִי הוּא (Genesis 20:13, \"say of me, he is my brother\"), וַיִּשְׁאֲלוּ אַנְשֵׁי הַמָּקוֹם לְאִשְׁתּוֹ (Genesis 26:7, \"and the men of the place asked about his wife\"), and many like these. In my opinion, since Moses mentioned in his blessing the name of Reuben and the name of Judah — יְחִי רְאוּבֵן (\"let Reuben live\"), שִׁמְעָה ה' קוֹל יְהוּדָה (Genesis 49:8 \"Hear, O YHWH, the voice of Judah\") — but would not mention the name of the rest of the tribes in their blessing, Scripture states וּלְלֵוִי אָמַר (\"and of Levi he said\"), לְבִנְיָמִין אָמַר (\"and of Benjamin he said\"), to indicate that the tribes stood before him, and he would call to Levi and set his eyes upon him and say: תֻּמֶּיךָ וְאוּרֶיךָ (\"your perfection and your Urim\"), and likewise each and every tribe he would bless face to face." "<b>Whom You tried at Massah</b> — whom You tested with a trial. And the reason is that he was a devout man (חָסִיד) in everything in which You tried him, and no fault was found in him, except for the waters of Meribah, for which You already punished him, because concerning the Calf he acted for the honor of HaShem. This is the language of Ibn Ezra (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 33:8). And in the Sifrei (Sifrei Beshalach §Shamet): \"אֲשֶׁר נִסִּיתוֹ בְּמַסָּה — You tried him many times and he was found whole in all the trials. תְּרִיבֵהוּ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה — סְקוּפִּים נִסְקַפְתָּ לוֹ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה (charges were framed against him concerning the waters of Meribah); if Moses said (Numbers 20:10) שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים (\"Hear now, you rebels\") — what did Aaron and Miriam do?\" And what seems correct in my eyes according to the plain sense is that <b>בְּמַסָּה</b>, in its plain meaning, is the place where they tested HaShem at Rephidim (Exodus 17:7). He says: \"Your תֻּמִּים and Your אוּרִים shall be forever for Your devout man and for his seed after him, whom You tried at Massah\" — when the children of Israel sinned and tested HaShem at the waters, he was not among the congregation that assembled there against HaShem, for he believed in HaShem and in His word, that He would bring forth water for them from the flinty rock. And the second time: תְּרִיבֵהוּ — You contended with him and punished him over the waters of Meribah of Israel, for because of their strife the punishment fell upon him, yet he did not act treacherously against HaShem, for even at first he believed that אַתָּה הַהֹפְכִי הַצּוּר אֲגַם מָיִם (\"You who turn the rock into a pool of water\"), all the more so this time, when he had already seen such a thing. Moses hints that that sin was on account of the people, for because of their strife Moses and Aaron thought they needed to do what they did, lest the Shekhinah withdraw on account of their sin. And this is what is said (Psalms 106:32): וַיַּקְצִיפוּ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה וַיֵּרַע לְמֹשֶׁה בַּעֲבוּרָם (\"And they provoked wrath at the waters of Meribah, and it went ill with Moses on their account\"). And the meaning of <b>תְּרִיבֵהוּ</b> is like רִיבָה ה' אֶת יְרִיבַי (Psalms 35:1, \"Contend, O YHWH, with those who contend with me\") — that You rendered him the judgment of the quarrel that the children of Israel waged against HaShem. And it is possible that its meaning is as stated in the Sifrei, that they are סְקוּפִּים, that is, accusations, hinting at the smallness of the sin and the greatness of the punishment, in the manner of בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ (Leviticus 10:3, \"through those near Me I will be sanctified\"). And so is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 33:8), who said: דְּנַסִּתוֹהִי בְּנִסֵּיתָא וְהֲוָה שְׁלִים, מֵרִיב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וּמִנַּסּוֹתָם אֶת ה' (\"You tried him in a trial and he was whole; from the strife of Israel and their testing of HaShem\"). Since he was whole the first time, all the more so the second time, for he believed in the word of HaShem, and You examined his heart at the waters of Meribah and found his heart faithful before You — for it is revealed and known before the Throne of Your Glory that he believes in You and in Your words, but on account of Israel the punishment fell upon him, as I explained in the verse גַּם בִּי הִתְאַנַּף ה' בִּגְלַלְכֶם (Deuteronomy 1:37, \"HaShem was also wrathful with me on your account\"). Now, at first he spoke concerning the priests, for he said of Levi that the Urim and Tumim would belong to His devout man among them, and afterward concerning the entire tribe — that You tried them with the Calf and in Egypt and in the wilderness, and therefore they shall teach His ordinances to Jacob. And so is the opinion of Onkelos, who said: כָּשְׁרִין אִלֵּין דְּיַלְּפוּן דִּינָךְ (\"these are worthy to teach Your laws\"), and so forth. Or he speaks concerning the future, as the words of Ibn Ezra: וְיֹרוּ מִשְׁפָּטֶיךָ — concerning all of them, וְיָשִׂימוּ קְטוֹרָה — concerning the priests among them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes שִׁמְעָה ה' קוֹל יְהוּדָה from Genesis 49:8 but the second citation in the Hebrew reads simply קוֹל יְהוּדָה; the full phrase is rendered here.", 1 "The Sifrei passage contains several Aramaic/Aramaized words (סְקוּפִּים, נִסְקַפְתָּ) that are rendered hyper-literally with parenthetical glosses, as their precise technical meaning in this midrashic context is debated among commentators."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:47.617086516Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}